---
// How AI sped up the physical design work: Fusion CAD through the Fusion MCP,
// export, merge with the physics simulation, and a Blender render.
// Hours with AI: active time in the Claude Code session logs, 20-23 Nov 2026
// (gaps over 10 min dropped; unattended renders not counted).
// Hours without AI: my own estimates.
// Figures: ORLO-Zig deliverables/ai_acceleration/make_diagrams.py (svg-figs skill).
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ShowFigure from '@components/ShowFigure.astro';

const work = [
  { name: 'First CAD-to-render pipeline', note: 'export, physics merge, Blender scene, render', ai: 3.8, est: 80 },
  { name: 'Scale-up to 1 m tiles', note: 'new CAD by script, new sim, new render', ai: 0.7, est: 40 },
  { name: 'Crawler built into the 49-tile robot', note: 'CAD fixes, early-sync render', ai: 1.3, est: 20 },
  { name: 'New design: racks in the tile corners', note: 'brainstorm to crawler v2', ai: 0.9, est: 16 },
];
const totAi = Math.round(work.reduce((s, w) => s + w.ai, 0) * 10) / 10;
const totEst = work.reduce((s, w) => s + w.est, 0);

const AI = '#0287b5';
const EST = '#c2410c';
const max = 80;
const plotW = 380;
const x = (h: number) => (h / max) * plotW;
const ticks = [0, 20, 40, 60, 80];

const mine = [
  'Designed the 49-tile assembler in Fusion: frame, gantry, end effector, tile shelf.',
  'Chose the McMaster parts: NEMA 17 motor (6627T65), module 1 pinion (2664N353), ball-bearing carriage (6709K114).',
  'Shaped the tiles, including a bottom extrusion so the straight tile sits in the storage shelf.',
  'Had the new ideas: racks in the tile corners, a crawler that climbs the stack, robots that sync after the first turn.',
  'Made the design calls: tile height near 30 mm, the channel rack, tiles placed next to two neighbours.',
];
const theirs = [
  'Read the whole assembly through the Fusion MCP (253 calls): parts, sizes, pinion geometry.',
  'Set up the rack and pinion rule as Fusion user parameters, so tiles stay whole teeth when resized.',
  'Rebuilt the assembler for 1 m tiles from a 438-line script: module 2, 159 teeth, new turn arcs.',
  'Added joints with travel limits and ran interference checks over the full travel.',
  'Exported 56 parts in groups that move together, merged them with the physics data, and wrote the Blender scene.',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Working with AI"
  description="How AI sped up the physical design: Fusion CAD through the Fusion MCP, merged with a physics simulation into a Blender render."
>
  <section class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-gradient-to-b from-arc-50/60 to-white">
    <div class="container-page pt-14 pb-12">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working with AI · CAD to render</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 max-w-3xl">From Fusion CAD to a physics-based render, with an AI agent</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-lg text-slate-600">
        I design the robot in Fusion 360 and make the design calls. An AI agent connected to Fusion
        (the Fusion MCP) edits the model, keeps the gear and joint relationships, exports the parts,
        merges them with the physics simulation and builds the Blender render. In four days we went
        from a CAD model to three rendered builds and one new crawler design.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid max-w-3xl grid-cols-2 gap-6 sm:grid-cols-4">
        <div><p class="stat m-0">≈{totAi} h</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">of agent sessions, 20–23 Nov</p></div>
        <div><p class="stat m-0">≈{totEst} h</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">my estimate without AI</p></div>
        <div><p class="stat m-0">253</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">Fusion edits and reads by the agent</p></div>
        <div><p class="stat m-0">56</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">CAD parts driven by the physics</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container-page pt-12 pb-8">
    <article class="article max-w-3xl">
      <section>
        <h2>1. The pipeline</h2>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="cad-pipeline" number={1} />
      </section>

      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2>2. What I did, and what the AI sped up</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="card border-t-4 p-5" style="border-top-color:#0a5f99">
            <p class="m-0 font-semibold" style="color:#0a5f99">Me</p>
            <ul class="mb-0 mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-6 text-slate-600">
              {mine.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card border-t-4 p-5" style="border-top-color:#a35d00">
            <p class="m-0 font-semibold" style="color:#a35d00">AI agent (Fusion MCP, Python, Blender)</p>
            <ul class="mb-0 mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-6 text-slate-600">
              {theirs.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="my-10">
          <div class="card p-5">
            <div class="mb-4 flex flex-wrap gap-5 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="inline-block h-3 w-3 rounded-sm" style={`background:${AI}`}></span>With AI (session logs)</span>
              <span class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="inline-block h-3 w-3 rounded-sm" style={`background:${EST}`}></span>Without AI (my estimate)</span>
            </div>
            {work.map((w) => (
              <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 py-1.5 sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:items-center">
                <div class="text-sm leading-5">
                  <span class="font-medium text-slate-800">{w.name}</span>
                  <span class="block text-xs text-slate-400">{w.note}</span>
                </div>
                <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${plotW + 60} 40`} class="block w-full" role="img" aria-label={`${w.name}: ${w.ai} h with AI, about ${w.est} h without`}>
                  {ticks.map((t) => <line x1={x(t)} x2={x(t)} y1="0" y2="40" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1" />)}
                  <g><title>{`${w.name}: ${w.ai} h with AI`}</title>
                    <rect x="0" y="3" width={Math.max(x(w.ai), 4)} height="14" rx="3" fill={AI} />
                    <rect x="0" y="0" width={plotW} height="20" fill="transparent" /></g>
                  <text x={Math.max(x(w.ai), 4) + 6} y="15" font-size="13" fill="#334155">{w.ai} h</text>
                  <g><title>{`${w.name}: about ${w.est} h without AI (estimate)`}</title>
                    <rect x="0" y="22" width={x(w.est)} height="14" rx="3" fill={EST} />
                    <rect x="0" y="20" width={plotW} height="20" fill="transparent" /></g>
                  <text x={x(w.est) + 6} y="34" font-size="13" fill="#334155">≈{w.est} h</text>
                </svg>
              </div>
            ))}
            <div class="hidden sm:grid sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr]">
              <span></span>
              <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${plotW + 60} 18`} class="block w-full" aria-hidden="true">
                {ticks.map((t) => <text x={x(t)} y="13" font-size="12" fill="#64748b" text-anchor={t === 0 ? 'start' : 'middle'}>{t} h</text>)}
              </svg>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-2.5 text-sm leading-6 text-slate-500">
            <span class="font-semibold text-slate-700">Figure 2. </span>
            Hours with AI are active time in the agent session logs ({totAi} h in all). Blender renders
            ran unattended and are not counted. Hours without AI are my own estimates for the same work,
            including learning the Fusion and Blender Python APIs. My own modelling time in Fusion is not
            in either bar.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2>3. Keeping the gears and joints right</h2>
        <p>
          One rule ties the design together: a tile edge must be a whole number of rack teeth. The agent
          stored it as Fusion parameters, so resizing a tile keeps the racks in step, and it reused the
          rule to scale the robot from 160 mm to 1 m tiles.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="rack-rule" number={3} />
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="gantry-closeup" number={4} />
      </section>

      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2>4. A new design in one evening</h2>
        <p>
          I proposed racks inside the tile corners so a crawler could climb the stack. The agent checked
          the tooth math, gave options, built it in Fusion, and then found that the crawler could twist,
          so it proposed a channel rack. About two hours from idea to a checked CAD model.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="brainstorm" number={5} />
      </section>

      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2>5. The render is driven by the physics</h2>
        <p>
          Every frame places the Fusion meshes where the simulation says. The structure's tilt comes from
          conservation of angular momentum as the robots and tiles move. The same scene also checks
          clearance, and it found the two robots overlap by up to 104 mm, before anything was built.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="blender-cad-pair" number={6} />
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="blender-two-robot-sync-early" number={7} />
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="blender-1m-tiles" number={8} />
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-500">
          Limits: the physics covers the free-floating rotation of the structure, not contact, gear
          meshing or flexing. Masses come from parts lists, not from Fusion's mass properties. For the
          1 m design those lists were measured off the Fusion model; for the 160 mm design many values
          are still estimates.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
